  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO \nCOUNTIES...\n\nAnother hot day is in store as high pressure remains over the \nSouthwest. Valley and coastal locations soaring into the 90s again \nthis morning as weak offshore winds hold across the area. Models \nshow better onshore flow (west winds) closer to the coast and far \nwestern valleys over the next couple of days. Still thinking \ntemperatures from today into Tuesday will be relatively similar. A \nsubtle increase in temperatures will be seen at the coast as a \nthermal ridge expands northward Wednesday into Thursday. This will \nmainly be felt across the mountains and the deserts with highs \nincreasing 3-5 degrees from where they are today. Low temperatures \nwill still remain warm over the region with many places staying into \nthe 70s overnight in the lower deserts and west of the mountains. \nLight offshore flow at night will be the main driver of low \ntemperatures. Wind-sheltered valleys will lower into the 60s in some \nareas, while communities that see wind the whole night will stay in \nthe mid 70s to near 80. Please continue to practice heat safety by \nfinding places with A/C and checking on friends and family. The \nmarine layer will also subtly build on Wednesday into Thursday, but \nconfidence is lower on when low clouds and fog reach coastal areas. \nForecast now shows fog possibly moving along the coast by Wednesday \nmorning. Chances for low clouds and fog will increase Thursday into \nFriday, and most definitely by the weekend for much of the coastal \nbasin.\n\nModels are starting to come into better agreement on when a trough \nof low pressure enters the Pacific Northwest, sometime later on \nFriday into early Saturday. This system will help weaken the high \npressure system over area. Height falls will lead to greater onshore \nflow and cooling. Highs from Thursday to Friday will drop another 1-\n4 degrees, with the greater cooling occurring from Friday to \nSaturday with highs lowering another 5-10 degrees. Friday will see \nhighs closer to 100 degrees for the lower deserts and inland coastal \nvalleys, with 80s more common near the coast. Models start to \ndiverge once again on where this trough will go and how far south it \nplunges across the western U.S. For now, another significant \ntemperature drop is in the forecast from Saturday to Sunday with \nhighs lowering another 5-10 degrees. On Sunday, there's around a 50-\n50 chance of temperatures staying below 80 degrees for the valleys \nwest of the mountains as well as the large cities of the high \ndesert. \n\nThe path of the weather system will determine how cool we become and \nif we are able to see any precipitation along and west of the \nmountains. Global models continue to show large discrepancies on how \nthis feature evolves. Taking a look at model ensembles, ECMWF brings \na wetter pattern with about 3/4 of the guidance showing light \nprecipitation while the GFS ensembles show a dry pattern with about \n1/4 of ensembles showing light precipitation Sunday through possibly \nnext Tuesday. Low confidence remains on any precipitation across the \nregion, but confidence is much higher in seeing cooler and breezier \nweather, along with low clouds each morning west of the mountains.",
